# The Joy of VEX -- Technical Reference

> Auto-generated from 8 code snippets

> Source: Matt Estela -- The Joy of VEX (YouTube)

---


## Houdini Hangout - 01/04 - The Joy of Vex (Days 1 & 2) (Episode 1)


### Color from Position [16:56]

*beginner | color | Attribute Wrangle*


```c
@Cd = @P;

// Variants:
// @Cd = @P;
// @Cd = @P.x;
```


Demonstrates assigning point position directly to color, where X, Y, Z coordinates map to R, G, B values. Since the geometry is centered around the origin, colors blend from negative (below black) through zero (black) to positive values. Using @P.x assigns only the X coordinate to all RGB channels, creating a grayscale gradient.


**Functions:** `@Cd`, `@P`


**Concepts:** vector to color mapping, RGB color space, position-based visualization, component access, coordinate systems


[Watch in video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=1016s)


---


### Mapping Attributes to Color [21:50]

*beginner | color | Attribute Wrangle*


```c
@Cd = @N.y;

// Alternative approaches:
// @Cd = @P;
// @Cd = @P.x;
```


Demonstrates assigning color attributes based on geometric properties. By setting @Cd equal to @N.y (the Y component of the normal), points receive a grayscale color ranging from 0 to 1 based on their normal direction. Points at the top of geometry with Y normals near 1.0 appear white, while those with lower Y values appear darker gray.


**Functions:** `@Cd`, `@N`, `@P`
